>>
endobj
300 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (An API is an Application Programming Interface that lets different software communicate with each other. The API I'm creating will let any application send questions to my RAG chatbot and receive responses, without needing to access the AWS Console or CLI. This will be helpful for integrating the chatbot into websites, mobile apps, or other tools, making it accessible and interactive for users everywhere.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5 6]
>>
endobj
301 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The API code has three main sections that work together to make the chatbot accessible. First, Imports bring in necessary libraries like FastAPI for the API framework, boto3 for AWS interactions, and dotenv/os for environment variables. Second, Environment Variables load your AWS region, Knowledge Base ID, and model ARN to connect the API to Bedrock. Third, Functions and API Endpoints define routes like the root page and /bedrock/query to handle requests and send them to the chatbot.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7 8]
>>
endobj
302 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (I set up the API by first cloning the GitHub repository using git clone [HTTPS-URL], which downloaded all the project files locally. Next, I created a virtual environment with python -m venv venv to keep dependencies isolated from my system Python. Finally, since I was on Windows, I activated the virtual environment using .\\venv\\Scripts\\activate)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [9 10]
>>
endobj
303 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (When I first tried running the API with python -m uvicorn main:app --reload, I got a ModuleNotFoundError: No module named uvicorn. This happened because the required dependencies were not installed in my virtual environment. To fix this, I ran pip3 install -r requirements.txt, which installed all the necessary Python packages like FastAPI and Uvicorn. After that, the API started without errors, and I could continue testing it.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [11 12]
>>
endobj
